'''Portnou International Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport Internacional de Portnou}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: PNU, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: PNIB) is the airport serving [[Portnou]] and the [[Marjal del Canal (comarca)|Marjal del Canal comarca]] in [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. Located in the neighbouring municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], approximately 7 km northwest of the Portnou city boundary, the airport serves domestic routes within Balboa and short-haul international flights. It is the second-busiest airport in Estret Province after [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]. The airport is also a base for [[Transabyala AirFreight]], a dedicated cargo airline serving the Lurandian and Abyalan regions.

The airport is served by the flag carrier [[Aeroístmus]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]]. It provides an alternative to Sant Cristòfor International for residents of Portnou and the southern Estret corridor, particularly for domestic connections to destinations not easily accessible by rail.

== History ==

=== Planning and construction ===

The need for an airport to serve Portnou and the Marjal del Canal comarca was identified in the mid-1980s, as the city's rapid post-reunification growth outpaced the capacity of the region's transport infrastructure. Portnou, with a population approaching 800,000 by the late 1980s, was the largest city in Balboa without its own airport, and residents travelling by air had to make the journey to [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] — approximately 90 minutes by road, or 30 minutes by HSR plus ground transfer.

A site in the neighbouring municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], on flat agricultural land approximately 7 km northwest of the Portnou city boundary, was selected in 1987. The site offered clear approaches over the coastal plain, proximity to the [[CristoCarril|Coastal Line]] rail corridor, and direct access to the [[E21 (Balboa)|E21]] expressway. Construction began in 1989, and the airport opened on 15 June 1992.

=== Early years and World Cup (1992–1994) ===

The airport opened with a single terminal and one runway (09/27), serving domestic routes operated by [[Aeroístmus]] and [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]]. The timing of the opening, two years before the [[1994 AFA World Cup]], was deliberate: Portnou was a host city for the tournament, and the airport was built in part to handle the expected influx of visitors. A second runway (15/33) was added in 1996 to accommodate growing traffic and to separate general aviation from commercial operations.

=== International services (2000s–present) ===

International services were introduced in 2003, when [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]] began flights to [[Tamsui International Airport|Tamsui]] and [[Gran Port de Sant Mateu International Airport|Gran Port de Sant Mateu]]. The international routes reflected the growing economic links between the Portnou port region and Lurandian and Abyalan destinations, and the increasing demand for direct international connections from the southern Estret corridor without transiting Sant Cristòfor.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminal ===

[[File:Portnou International Airport terminal.png|thumb|250px|The terminal building at Portnou International Airport]]
<!-- Image prompt: A regional international airport terminal in a tropical setting. Single-storey modern terminal building with glass facade and a curved roof, control tower visible behind. Palm trees and tropical vegetation around the car park and approach road. A small passenger aircraft on the apron visible through the glass. Warm tropical light, hazy atmosphere. Low-angle view showing the terminal entrance. -->

The airport has a single terminal with 12 gates, handling both domestic and international operations. The terminal was designed with a single concourse extending from a central check-in and baggage claim area, with domestic gates on one side and international gates on the other. The terminal has an annual capacity of approximately 5 million passengers.

=== Runways ===

[[File:Portnou International Airport runways aerial.png|thumb|250px|Aerial view of the two parallel runways at Portnou International Airport]]
<!-- Image prompt: Aerial view of a regional airport in a tropical lowland setting. Two parallel runways aligned at an angle matching the nearby E21 expressway, terminal building and apron visible between them. Flat coastal plain with tropical vegetation around the airport perimeter. Hazy tropical light, wide-angle aerial view. -->

The airport has two parallel runways aligned on the same axis as the [[E21 (Balboa)|E21]] expressway:

